The '''demographics of Balboa''' are characterised by the country's complex racial history, its position as a crossroads between Illypnia, Fosia, and Kaftia, and the legacy of the [[Twelve Guarantees]], which established the legal framework for the post-reunification multi-ethnic state. The population of approximately 35.2 million is divided among five principal ethnic groups — [[Black Balboan people|Black]], [[White Balboan people|White]], [[Mixed Balboan people|Mixed]], [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian]], and [[Birú people|Birú]] — whose boundaries have been shaped by centuries of colonial rule, racial legislation, immigration, and post-reunification reform.

== Population ==

Balboa's population is approximately 35,234,000 (2026 estimate). The population density is approximately 119 per square kilometre, though the distribution is highly uneven: the Shendan coastal plain, which includes [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the canal corridor, and the Aethiopian coastal plain, which includes [[Sant Llàtzer]] and the canal's eastern terminus, are significantly more densely populated than the interior highlands. [[Estret Province]], which contains the capital and the canal corridor, accounts for approximately 41 per cent of the national population on approximately 20 per cent of the land area; [[Tramuntana Province]], the northernmost and least populous province, contains approximately 5 per cent of the population on approximately 18 per cent of the land area.

The population growth rate is approximately 0.8 per cent (2025), driven by natural increase and a modest net immigration inflow. Fertility rates vary by province and ethnicity: the national total fertility rate is approximately 2.1 children per woman (2025), near replacement level, though rates in the interior provinces and among the Birú community are higher, while rates in the urban Estret corridor are lower. Life expectancy at birth is approximately 76.8 years (2025), with a significant gap between Estret (79.2 years) and Tramuntana (72.4 years).

