The official language of Balboa is [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], the language of the former colonial power, which is used in government, education, the judiciary, and formal commerce. Volisanian is spoken as a first language by approximately 40 per cent of the population, primarily white and mixed Balboans, and as a second language by the majority of the remainder.

[[Balboan Creole]], a creole language derived from Volisanian with significant Kaftian and Fosian lexical influences, is the most widely spoken vernacular. It is the first language of the majority of Black Balboans and a substantial portion of the mixed population, and it functions as the lingua franca of everyday life in the cities and the coastal provinces. Balboan Creole was standardised in 2018 with an orthography based on the [[Echian script]], though it has no official status and Volisanian remains the sole language of government and formal administration. Code-switching between Volisanian and Creole is common across all ethnic groups.

The [[Zong language]] is spoken within the Fosian community, and is recognised as a minority language under the Twelve Guarantees' cultural protections. Zong is the primary language of the Zong- and Sinchewese-descended communities in Sant Cristòfor, the canal corridor, and Migjorn Province. The language is taught in community-run schools, and public signage in Fosian-majority neighbourhoods is bilingual.

The [[Birú Dale language]], the ancestral language of the Birú people, is critically endangered. It is classified as a threatened language with declining intergenerational transmission. Fewer than 20,000 people speak Birú Dale fluently, nearly all of them over the age of sixty, and the language is not used in education or public life. The Birú community and several cultural organisations have called for Birú Dale to be recognised as a national heritage language and for immersion programmes to be established in Birú communities, but these proposals have not been implemented.

The national literacy rate is approximately 95 per cent, with near-universal literacy among those under forty.

== Religion ==

[[File:Tianist temple Sant Cristofor.jpg|thumb|250px|A Tianist temple in the Fosian quarter of Sant Cristòfor]]

Balboa is religiously pluralistic, with no single denomination commanding a majority of the population. The religious landscape reflects the country's colonial history (Catholicism), its Fosian immigration (Tianism), and the secularising effects of the post-reunification period.

