[[Catholicism]], the legacy of the Volisanian colonial period, remains the largest single denomination at approximately 40 per cent of the population. It is practised by the majority of the white and mixed populations and by a significant portion of the Black population, particularly in the rural provinces and the interior highlands. The [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] is the country's principal Catholic see.

[[Tianism]], brought by the Fosian community, is the second-largest religion at approximately 25 per cent. It is practised by the majority of the Fosian population and by a small but growing number of converts from other groups, particularly in the cities. Tianist temples are a prominent feature of the urban landscape in Sant Cristòfor, Portnou, and Novara.

[[Protestantism]] has a modest presence at approximately 10 per cent, primarily among some urban communities. Protestant denominations, including evangelical and Pentecostal churches, have grown slowly since the mid-twentieth century.

Approximately 20 per cent of the population identifies as having no religious affiliation, a proportion that has increased significantly since the reunification, particularly among the urban professional class and younger generations.

Indigenous and syncretic religious traditions, including practices that blend Catholic, Tianist, and Birú elements, persist at approximately 5 per cent, primarily in the rural interior and among the Birú community. These traditions are not formally organised and are often practised alongside or within the framework of Catholicism.

== Education ==

The national literacy rate is approximately 95 per cent (2026), with near-universal literacy among those under forty. Educational attainment varies significantly by province and ethnicity. In Estret Province, approximately 42 per cent of the adult population has completed post-secondary education; in Tramuntana, the figure is approximately 12 per cent. The Birú community has the lowest rates of educational attainment in Balboa, with limited access to secondary and post-secondary education in the remote highland districts.

The [[Twelve Guarantees]] established the right to Zong-language and Balboan Creole-language education in public schools, and the Zong-language school system, which had been maintained by the Fosian community since the Zong Balboa period, was incorporated into the public education system. The Guarantees also mandated educational scholarships for descendants of enslaved persons as part of the reparations programme.

== Health ==

Life expectancy at birth is approximately 76.8 years (2025), with a significant gap between Estret Province (79.2 years) and Tramuntana Province (72.4 years). The infant mortality rate is approximately 14 per 1,000 live births (2025), with higher rates in the interior provinces. Healthcare access is strongly correlated with geography: the canal corridor has modern hospitals and clinics, while the interior highlands have limited healthcare infrastructure, particularly in the remote Birú communities.

The [[Twelve Guarantees]] established the right to healthcare as part of the broader civil rights protections, and the post-reunification BPP government invested heavily in healthcare infrastructure. However, the geographic disparities in access persist, and the Birú community continues to face significant health challenges, including higher rates of infectious disease, maternal mortality, and malnutrition.

